Simplest test: netcat (raw TCP, no HTTP/TLS involved)

On the Linux VM (listener):

bash
nc -lvp 5000

On the Windows VM (client):
If you have WSL or Git Bash:

bash
nc -v <linux-vm-ip> 5000

Or with PowerShell (no netcat needed):

powershell
$client = New-Object System.Net.Sockets.TcpClient("<linux-vm-ip>", 5000)
$stream = $client.GetStream()
Write-Host "Connected at $(Get-Date)"
while ($client.Connected) {
    Start-Sleep -Seconds 30
    Write-Host "Still connected at $(Get-Date)"
}
Write-Host "Disconnected at $(Get-Date)"

Type nothing, just leave both sides open and idle. Note the exact timestamp when the connection drops. That's your real idle timeout, independent of app/HTTPS logic.

Better test: with packet capture running simultaneously

Run this alongside the above so you catch how it dies (RST vs silent drop vs FIN):

bash
# On Linux VM, in a separate terminal
sudo tcpdump -i any host <windows-vm-ip> and port 5000 -w idle_test.pcap

After it drops, check the capture:

bash
tcpdump -r idle_test.pcap -n

Look for a R (RST) flag near the failure timestamp, and note which IP sent it — that tells you whether it's the Linux box, the Windows box, or a device in between (if the RST source IP is neither VM's own IP, it's a device on the path spoofing/relaying).

Even more precise: TCP keepalive probe timing test

To find the exact idle threshold rather than just confirming ~15 min, script incrementing waits:

PowerShell — test at 5, 10, 12, 14, 15, 16 min:

powershell
$times = @(300, 600, 720, 840, 900, 960) # seconds
foreach ($t in $times) {
    $client = New-Object System.Net.Sockets.TcpClient("<linux-ip>", 5000)
    Write-Host "Connected, waiting $t seconds..."
    Start-Sleep -Seconds $t
    try {
        $client.Client.Send([byte[]](0)) | Out-Null
        Write-Host "$t sec: still alive"
    } catch {
        Write-Host "$t sec: DEAD - $($_.Exception.Message)"
    }
    $client.Close()
}
